<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ioane Petritsi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th–12th centuries</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ioane Petritsi was a Georgian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonist</a> philosopher and translator of the late eleventh and early twelfth centuries. After working in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bachkovo-monastery">Petritsoni</a> monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, he is traditionally said to have taught at the Gelati academy. He translated works of Proclus and Nemesius into Georgian, and his commentary on Proclus's Elements of Theology is a major work of medieval Georgian thought.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ioane-petritsi.jpg" alt="Ioane Petritsi" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LeontinaVarlamonva · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monument_dedicated_to_the_11th_century_Georgian_philosopher_John_Petritsi_in_Tbilisi,_Georgia_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
